from lib import *
C=9;S='maths'
CH='algebra'
lesson(C,S,CH,'polynomials-and-remainder-theorem',"Polynomials, zeros and the Remainder Theorem","The cost formula","A shop charges 5x squared + 3x + 2 rupees for an order of x items on a certain plan. Expressions like this are polynomials, and we often need to know when they equal zero.",
["A polynomial in x is an expression with whole-number powers of x, like 3x squared - 5x + 7. Its degree is the highest power. @ polynomial",
 "Polynomials of degree 1, 2, 3 are called linear, quadratic and cubic. With one, two or three terms they are monomial, binomial or trinomial. @ degree and types",
 "A zero of p(x) is a number a for which p(a) = 0. @ zero of a polynomial",
 "A linear polynomial ax + b has exactly one zero, x = -b/a. @ -b/a",
 "Remainder Theorem: when p(x) is divided by (x - a), the remainder is p(a). @ remainder = p(a)",
 "Factor Theorem: (x - a) is a factor of p(x) exactly when p(a) = 0. @ factor theorem",
 "So to test whether (x - 2) divides x cubed - 8, evaluate p(2) = 8 - 8 = 0, so it does. @ test with p(a)"],
("Find the remainder when p(x) = x cubed - 3x squared + 4x - 5 is divided by (x - 2).",[("By the Remainder Theorem the remainder is p(2).","Divisor x - 2 means a = 2."),("p(2) = 8 - 12 + 8 - 5.","Substitute x = 2."),("Remainder = -1.","8 - 12 = -4, then -4 + 8 = 4, then 4 - 5 = -1.")]),
("State the Remainder Theorem and the Factor Theorem.",["Remainder on dividing by x - a is p(a)","Factor if p(a) = 0","How to find zeros"],["Using p(-a) instead of p(a)"]),
["The degree is the highest power of the variable.","Zero of ax + b is -b/a.","Remainder on dividing by (x - a) is p(a).","(x - a) is a factor if p(a) = 0."],
["n|Degree of 4x^3 - 2x + 1 is|3|Highest power of x.",
 "m|Zero of 2x - 6 is|3|-3~Solve 2x = 6;6~That is the constant term|x = 6/2.",
 "t|If p(2) = 0 then (x - 2) is a factor of p(x).|True|Factor theorem.",
 "n|Remainder when x^2 + 3x + 2 is divided by (x - 1) is|6|p(1) = 1 + 3 + 2.",
 "t|x^2 + 1/x is a polynomial.|False|Powers must be non-negative whole numbers.",
 "n|p(x) = x^2 - 5x + 6. Find p(3).|0|9 - 15 + 6.",
 "n|Number of zeros of a linear polynomial|1|ax + b = 0 has one solution.",
 "s|A polynomial of degree 2 is called ____.|quadratic|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'identities-and-factorisation',"Algebraic identities and factorisation","The square garden","A square garden has side (a + b). What is its area? Splitting it into four rectangles shows (a + b) squared = a squared + 2ab + b squared - and this is an identity.",
["An identity is true for every value of the variables. @ identity",
 "(a + b) squared = a squared + 2ab + b squared and (a - b) squared = a squared - 2ab + b squared. @ square identities",
 "a squared - b squared = (a + b)(a - b). @ difference of squares",
 "(x + a)(x + b) = x squared + (a + b)x + ab. @ (x + a)(x + b)",
 "(a + b + c) squared = a squared + b squared + c squared + 2ab + 2bc + 2ca. @ trinomial square",
 "a cubed + b cubed = (a + b)(a squared - ab + b squared) and a cubed - b cubed = (a - b)(a squared + ab + b squared). @ sum and difference of cubes",
 "To factorise, look first for a common factor, then try an identity, then split the middle term. @ factorise steps"],
("Factorise x squared + 7x + 12 and evaluate 103 squared quickly.",[("Look for two numbers with product 12 and sum 7: 3 and 4.","Split the middle term."),("x squared + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4).","Check: 3 x 4 = 12, 3 + 4 = 7."),("103 squared = (100 + 3) squared = 10000 + 600 + 9 = 10609.","Use (a + b) squared.")]),
("List the three most common identities and how to use them to factorise.",["(a+b)^2, (a-b)^2, a^2 - b^2","Common factor first","Split the middle term"],["Writing (a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2"]),
["(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.","a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b).","(x + a)(x + b) = x^2 + (a+b)x + ab.","Factorise: common factor, identity, split the middle term."],
["m|(a + b)^2 =|a^2 + 2ab + b^2|a^2 + b^2~The middle term is missing;a^2 + ab + b^2~It is 2ab|Standard identity.",
 "n|99 squared using (100 - 1)^2 is|9801|10000 - 200 + 1.",
 "t|a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b).|True|Difference of squares.",
 "m|x^2 - 9 factorises as|(x - 3)(x + 3)|(x - 9)(x + 1)~Check by expanding;(x - 3)^2~That gives x^2 - 6x + 9|a^2 - b^2.",
 "n|(x + 2)(x + 5) has x term coefficient|7|2 + 5.",
 "t|(a + b)^2 = a^2 + b^2.|False|The 2ab term is missing.",
 "n|If a + b = 5 and ab = 6, a^2 + b^2 =|13|25 - 12.",
 "s|An equation true for all values of the variables is an ____.|identity|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'linear-equations-in-two-variables',"Linear equations in two variables and their graphs","The two parcels","A courier charges Rs 10 plus Rs 5 per kg. The cost y = 5x + 10 relates weight x and cost y. Each (x, y) pair that fits is a solution; together they lie on a straight line.",
["A linear equation in two variables has the form ax + by + c = 0 with a and b not both zero. @ ax + by + c = 0",
 "It has infinitely many solutions (x, y); each solution is a point on its graph. @ infinitely many solutions",
 "To graph it, find at least two (better three) solutions, plot them and draw the straight line through them. @ plot and join",
 "The line x = 0 is the y-axis and y = 0 is the x-axis; x = k is a vertical line and y = k a horizontal line. @ x = k, y = k",
 "Putting x = 0 gives the y-intercept, and y = 0 gives the x-intercept. @ intercepts",
 "A point lies on the line only if its coordinates satisfy the equation. @ check a point",
 "Two such equations together make a system, and their common solution is where the two lines meet. @ system"],
("Draw the graph of 2x + y = 4.",[("If x = 0 then y = 4, so (0, 4) is on the line. If y = 0 then x = 2, so (2, 0) is on the line.","Intercepts give two quick points."),("A third point: x = 1 gives y = 2, so (1, 2) lies on it too.","A third point checks the line."),("Plot the three points; they lie on one straight line. Draw it with arrowheads.","The line extends in both directions.")]),
("How do you draw the graph of a linear equation in two variables?",["Find two or three solutions","Plot the points","Join with a straight line"],["Plotting only one point"]),
["ax + by + c = 0 has infinitely many solutions.","Its graph is a straight line.","x = 0 gives the y-intercept; y = 0 the x-intercept.","A point on the line satisfies the equation."],
["m|Which point lies on x + y = 5?|(2, 3)|(1, 3)~1 + 3 = 4;(5, 5)~Sum is 10|2 + 3 = 5.",
 "n|If y = 2x + 1 and x = 3, y =|7|2 x 3 + 1.",
 "t|The graph of a linear equation in two variables is a straight line.|True|By definition.",
 "n|y-intercept of 3x + y = 6 is|6|Put x = 0.",
 "n|x-intercept of 2x + y = 8 is|4|Put y = 0: 2x = 8.",
 "t|x = 5 is a vertical line.|True|Every point on it has x = 5.",
 "t|A linear equation in two variables has exactly one solution.|False|It has infinitely many solutions.",
 "s|The point where a graph crosses the y-axis is the y-____.|intercept|Definition."],
svg=(grid([(0,4,'(0,4)'),(1,2,'(1,2)'),(2,0,'(2,0)')],-2,5,[(-1,6,3,-2)]),"Graph of the line 2x + y = 4 through (0,4), (1,2) and (2,0)","A straight line graph"))
CH='geometry'
lesson(C,S,CH,'quadrilaterals-and-parallelograms',"Quadrilaterals and parallelograms","The tiled floor","Floor tiles are often parallelograms or rectangles. Their shape rules tell us why they fit together without gaps.",
["A quadrilateral has four sides and four angles, and the sum of its interior angles is 360 degrees. @ 360 degrees",
 "A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel. Its opposite sides are equal and opposite angles are equal. @ parallelogram",
 "The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. @ diagonals bisect",
 "A rectangle is a parallelogram with all angles 90 degrees and equal diagonals. @ rectangle",
 "A rhombus has four equal sides and its diagonals bisect at right angles. @ rhombus",
 "A square is both a rectangle and a rhombus. @ square",
 "A trapezium has one pair of parallel sides; the mid-point theorem says the segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half of it. @ mid-point theorem"],
("In a parallelogram ABCD, angle A = 70 degrees. Find the other angles.",[("Opposite angles are equal, so angle C = 70 degrees.","Property of parallelograms."),("Adjacent angles add to 180 degrees: angle B = 180 - 70 = 110 degrees.","Co-interior angles between parallel lines."),("Angle D = 110 degrees too.","Opposite to B.")]),
("List three properties of a parallelogram and how a rhombus differs.",["Opposite sides and angles equal","Diagonals bisect each other","Rhombus has equal sides and perpendicular diagonals"],["Thinking all parallelograms have equal diagonals"]),
["Angle sum of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees.","Parallelogram: opposite sides/angles equal; diagonals bisect each other.","Rhombus: four equal sides; diagonals at right angles.","Mid-point theorem: segment = half of the third side."],
["n|Sum of the angles of a quadrilateral (degrees)|360|Two triangles of 180 degrees.",
 "t|Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal.|True|Standard property.",
 "m|The diagonals of a rhombus|bisect at right angles|are always equal~That is a rectangle;are parallel~They intersect|Rhombus property.",
 "n|If one angle of a parallelogram is 65 degrees, its adjacent angle is|115|180 - 65.",
 "t|Every square is a rhombus.|True|All sides are equal.",
 "t|Every rectangle is a square.|False|A rectangle's sides need not be equal.",
 "n|Mid-points of two sides of a triangle are joined. The segment is half of the third side of length 10, so its length is|5|Mid-point theorem.",
 "s|A quadrilateral with only one pair of parallel sides is a ____.|trapezium|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'circles-chords-and-cyclic-quadrilaterals',"Circles, chords and cyclic quadrilaterals","The Ferris wheel","Seats on a Ferris wheel lie on a circle. Joining some seats gives chords and quadrilaterals whose angles obey neat rules.",
["The distance from the centre to any point on the circle is the radius; a chord joins two points on the circle; the longest chord is the diameter. @ radius, chord, diameter",
 "The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord. @ perpendicular bisects chord",
 "Equal chords are equally distant from the centre, and chords equidistant from the centre are equal. @ equal chords",
 "The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc. @ centre angle = 2 x",
 "Angles in the same segment are equal, and the angle in a semicircle is a right angle. @ same segment, semicircle",
 "A cyclic quadrilateral has all four vertices on a circle, and its opposite angles add up to 180 degrees. @ opposite angles 180",
 "The converse holds: if the opposite angles of a quadrilateral add to 180 degrees, it is cyclic. @ converse"],
("In a cyclic quadrilateral ABCD angle A = 80 degrees and angle B = 100 degrees. Find angles C and D.",[("Opposite angles add to 180: angle C = 180 - 80 = 100 degrees.","A and C are opposite."),("Angle D = 180 - 100 = 80 degrees.","B and D are opposite."),("Check the total: 80 + 100 + 100 + 80 = 360 degrees.","Angle sum of a quadrilateral.")]),
("State the key angle properties of a circle.",["Perpendicular from centre bisects a chord","Angle at centre is twice angle at circumference","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180"],["Confusing diameter with radius"]),
["Perpendicular from the centre bisects the chord.","Angle at the centre = 2 x angle at the circumference.","Angle in a semicircle = 90 degrees.","Cyclic quadrilateral: opposite angles add to 180 degrees."],
["n|The angle in a semicircle (degrees) is|90|Standard theorem.",
 "t|The longest chord of a circle is its diameter.|True|It passes through the centre.",
 "n|The angle at the circumference when the angle at the centre is 120 degrees is|60|Half of 120.",
 "n|In a cyclic quadrilateral one angle is 70 degrees. The opposite angle is|110|180 - 70.",
 "t|Opposite angles of any quadrilateral add to 180 degrees.|False|Only for cyclic quadrilaterals.",
 "m|The perpendicular from the centre to a chord|bisects the chord|doubles the chord~Not a property;is equal to the radius~Not generally|Standard theorem.",
 "n|Radius 5 cm; the diameter in cm is|10|2 x 5.",
 "s|A quadrilateral with all vertices on a circle is a ____ quadrilateral.|cyclic|Definition."])
